15:31:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The short answer: the internet didn’t “break” on a single, dramatic day, and it won’t suddenly stop working. Instead, we’ve been managing a gradual, multi-layered transition for decades. Here’s what’s actually happening and what it means. --- ### 1. We ran out a while ago—just not all at once The central pool of fresh IPv4 addresses, managed by IANA, was exhausted in 2011 when the last five big blocks were handed out to the five Regional Internet Registries (RIRs) around the world. Since then, the RIRs have been doling out their final supplies under strict policies. Most of them have now hit “phase 3” or “phase 4” exhaustion, meaning they have essentially no free blocks to give to new network operators. For example: - ARIN (North America) ran out of its normal free pool in 2015. - RIPE NCC (Europe, Middle East, parts of Central Asia) ran out in 2019. - APNIC (Asia-Pacific) reached final /8 policies way back in 2011. So, **the IPv4 run‑out isn’t in the future—it’s already happened**, and the internet has been adapting. --- ### 2. What do we do when we can’t get “new” public addresses? Because the world’s networks still overwhelmingly run on IPv4, we rely on a combination of technical workarounds and market economics: #### a) Network Address Translation (NAT) Most homes and businesses have been behind a NAT router for years. One public IP is shared among all devices, using private addresses (192.168.x.x, 10.x.x.x) inside. This extended the life of IPv4 dramatically. #### b) Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT) When an ISP runs out of public addresses for its customers, it puts many of them behind a second layer of NAT at the ISP level. You share a public IPv4 address with dozens or hundreds of other subscribers. This is common on fixed wireless, some DSL, and many mobile networks. *Consequences for users under CGNAT:* - You cannot host services (game servers, personal websites, IP cameras) using just IPv4 without workarounds. - Peer-to-peer applications (some games, VoIP, BitTorrent) can suffer without relay servers (STUN/TURN) or IPv6 support. - Some online services might see misbehaviour if many users come from the same IP (like rate-limiting). #### c) Address trading and leasing IPv4 addresses are now a commodity. Companies that have more than they need sell surplus blocks on transfer markets. Prices have risen to around $30–$50 per address, making a “/24” (256 addresses) cost thousands of dollars. This makes it expensive for new ISPs, hosting providers, or cloud platforms to grow. It also incentivizes consolidation and more efficient use. #### d) Cloud providers start charging for public IPv4 AWS began charging $0.005 per hour per public IPv4 address in 2024 (about $3.65/month). Other cloud providers have followed suit. This is a direct result of scarcity—they can’t just get endless blocks cheaply anymore. --- ### 3. The real long‑term solution: IPv6 IPv6 with its 128‑bit addresses (340 undecillion of them) was designed as the permanent fix, and its adoption is accelerating because of the v4 shortage. - **Mobile networks** are leading: T‑Mobile US, Reliance Jio, and many others run IPv6‑only internally, using NAT64/DNS64 to reach IPv4‑only sites. Over 90% of T‑Mobile’s data traffic is IPv6. - **Dual stack** (running both IPv4 and IPv6) is the mainstream transition method for content providers, ISPs, and enterprises. Today, around 40‑45% of Google’s users access the service over IPv6 (and that’s growing). - **IPv4‑as‑a‑service** (464XLAT, MAP‑T, etc.) lets ISPs give customers only IPv6, then translate to IPv4 for legacy connections. As more networks and content move to IPv6, the pressure on IPv4 will slowly ease, but we’ll be living with both protocols for decades. --- ### 4. What actually happens to you? **For the average home user** already behind a legacy CPE (customer‑premises equipment)? You might not notice much. Your ISP has already deployed NAT; sites still load. The subtle issues (like broken gaming or VoIP) are often solved with relays or by the applications themselves using IPv6 where available. **For business and new entrants:** - Getting a new block of provider‑independent IPv4 addresses is near‑impossible or very expensive. - Planning for IPv6 is no longer optional—it’s a business requirement for growth, new deployments, and IoT. - Legacy IPv4‑only services (older websites, DNS servers, email servers) will gradually become unreachable by users who are IPv6‑only, but this is being monitored carefully. **For the internet’s structure:** - We’ll see an increasingly fragmented IPv4 world, with heavy reliance on intermediaries (proxies, load balancers, CDNs using shared pools). - IPv4 will eventually become the legacy “second‑class” network, with IPv6 carrying the majority of modern traffic. --- ### Bottom line Running out of IPv4 addresses never meant a catastrophic collapse. Instead, it’s a slow‑motion scarcity that has triggered a global migration to NAT, a market for used addresses, and—most importantly—a long‑overdue shift to IPv6. The internet is adapting layer by layer, and for most people, the experience remains seamless. The big change is behind the scenes: the cost and availability of public IPv4 are forever altered, and the future is increasingly IPv6‑first.
