ough a transfer from an organization that already has it. That’s exactly the definition of “run out.” So when I say we’ve run out, I’m simply reporting this state of affairs. The cupboards are bare. There’s no ambiguity there. --- ### Why, then, has everyone been talking about shortage for years? Because the effects of that emptiness took time to filter down to ordinary people and businesses. The warnings started decades ago, but the actual pain arrived in waves: - **2011–2015:** The registries themselves ran out. Large ISPs and legacy holders still had stockpiles, so most users didn’t feel it. But the alarm bells were loud, and news coverage was heavy. - **Late 2010s:** Those stockpiles began running low. ISPs started deploying Carrier‑Grade NAT, and new cloud providers couldn’t get the big blocks they needed. The “IPv4 shortage” headlines then reflected the growing market cost. - **Now (2021‑onward):** The market has tightened even more. Cloud providers (AWS, Azure, Google) now charge for public IPv4. Small ISPs and startups can’t afford to buy blocks at $30‑50 per address. This is when your friend’s company felt it directly. So you’ve been hearing about it for a long time because the crisis is slow‑burning. It was first an abstract registry exhaustion, then a slow drying‑up of internal caches, and now a direct financial and technical hurdle for anyone needing new addresses. The talk didn’t die down because the situation kept getting worse, not better. --- ### Your friend’s experience is the living proof You asked “Are you sure we haven’t run out?”—and your friend’s trouble is the very evidence. If free pools still existed, their company could have just requested a new block from the registry like the old days. The fact that they couldn’t and had to struggle (likely by going to the transfer market) **is** what “run out” means in practice. You’re witnessing the after‑effects right now. So I’m certain not because of a belief, but because: - The official registries publicly declared exhaustion years ago. - The only way to get space now is a commercial transaction between entities, which wouldn’t happen if there were still free addresses. - Countless engineers just like your friend encounter this exact problem daily. The long‑running chatter doesn’t make the current state false—it makes it the predicted, long‑anticipated outcome that finally caught up to the real world. Does that help make sense of the certainty and the paradox of the never‑ending warnings?
